To: Supply Chain Leadership Team
From: Sustainability & Logistics Director
Subject: Balanced shipping plan to manage cost and carbon across our warehouse-to-store network
Date: 

Context
We move finished goods from multiple warehouses to multiple stores. Each warehouse–store route has two performance measures: a monetary cost per unit shipped and a carbon emission rate per unit. In practice, the lowest-cost lanes often carry higher emissions. To support both margin and our sustainability commitments, we will take an integrated approach that evaluates these two measures together, rather than in isolation.

Operational rules we must honor
- Route setup/activation costs: Opening any route incurs a one-time setup expense tied to contracting, customs handling, and dock preparation. A route is either active or inactive; once activated, this setup expense is charged regardless of volume moved.
- Minimum order quantities on active routes: If we activate a route, we must ship at least a defined minimum number of units on that lane to justify fixed handling and ensure full-load efficiency. Partial loads below the minimum are not permitted.
- Supplier limits per store: To manage receiving capacity, vendor oversight, and service consistency at the store, each location can be served by no more than a limited number of warehouses. This pushes us to consolidate inbound flows and avoid fragmenting deliveries across too many sources.

Supply and demand guardrails
- Warehouse supply limits: Shipments out of each warehouse cannot exceed its available inventory.
- Store demand requirements: Each store’s minimum demand must be satisfied through the activated routes that serve it.

Management priority: balancing cost and emissions
Leadership has set importance weights to reflect how much we value lower variable cost relative to lower emissions. We will combine the two per-unit measures using these agreed weights to produce a single blended score for each feasible plan. Route setup costs will be added to that score at full value and will not be part of the weighting. This ensures we account for both ongoing variable costs and the real, one-time expense of opening lanes.

Business goal
Identify which warehouse–store routes to activate and how much to ship on each activated route so that:
- No warehouse ships more than its available inventory.
- Each store’s demand is fully met.
- Any activated route carries at least its minimum order quantity.
- Each store is served by no more than the allowed number of warehouses.
- The resulting plan achieves the most favorable blend of total variable cost and total emissions according to management’s weights, plus all applicable setup costs.

Next steps
With the attached data template, we will evaluate feasible network configurations, quantify the trade-offs between cheaper but higher-emission lanes and cleaner but higher-cost lanes, and recommend a shipping plan that satisfies operations and service rules while delivering the best blended outcome under current priorities. We will also prepare sensitivity views to illustrate how the plan shifts if the balance between cost and emissions changes.

Annex — Data Template
- Number of source warehouses: {NUM_SOURCES}
- Number of destination stores: {NUM_DESTINATIONS}
- Warehouse supplies (units available per warehouse): {SUPPLIES}
- Store demands (units required per store): {DEMANDS}
- Route variable cost per unit ($/unit) [warehouse × store matrix]: {COST_MATRIX}
- Route emissions per unit (kg CO2/unit) [warehouse × store matrix]: {EMISSION_MATRIX}
- Importance weight on cost (share of blended score): {WEIGHT_COST}
- Importance weight on emissions (share of blended score): {WEIGHT_EMISSION}
- Route setup/activation costs ($ per route): {FIXED_COSTS}
- Minimum order quantities per active route (units): {MIN_ORDER_QUANTITIES}
- Maximum number of supplying warehouses per store: {MAX_SUPPLIERS_PER_STORE}
